WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.500
[Tone]

00:00:01.300 --> 00:01:06.160
[Music]

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:10.780
[Narrator:] These are the wounded, sick, and
injured of the Navy.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:15.120
They are being taken from wherever they were
hurt or fell sick, to naval hospitals where

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:18.100
they will get the best possible medical care.

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:36.860
[Music]

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:42.160
These men are suffering with everything from
wounds and burns received in battle,

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:44.920
to broken ankles from falling off a ladder cleaning windows.

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:49.140
Some are sick from physical causes, like heart
trouble or pneumonia.

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:55.180
Others are just as sick from mental causes,
the N.P. or neuro-psychiatric patients.

00:01:55.800 --> 00:02:00.560
All sick people require treatment, but right
now you're chiefly interested in the treatment of the mentally ill.

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:05.540
These men are being admitted to the N.P. ward.

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:08.680
For the most part, they appear more or less
normal.

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:13.280
But if you look carefully, you will notice
that some of them show definite signs of their illness.

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:16.220
These patients are really sick.

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:19.299
Otherwise the doctors would have never sent
them to the hospital.

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:24.280
As hospital corpsmen, you will help in the
treatment of many types of mental illness,

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:27.360
and each one requires a different approach.

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:39.760
[Music]

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:44.460
[Speaker 1:] All right Miller, let's have
your wristwatch and wallet, because we're

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:45.820
going to give you a shower.

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:48.620
[Miller:] I lost my portable radio already.

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:53.980
[Speaker 1:] Well, all your stuff will be
put in this bag, which is numbered,

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:55.500
and then kept in a safe locker.

00:02:56.320 --> 00:03:01.200
Everything you leave with us is listed right
down here, so we have a complete record.

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:03.620
[Miller:] But what about that radio?

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:07.120
[Speaker 1:] Oh, well I'll make a note of
that right now and I'll have the chief

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:08.320
send out a tracer on it.

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:11.959
And now Miller, let's get that uniform off.

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:14.920
A shower'll feel good after three days on
the train.

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:19.920
[Narrator:] Treatment of an N.P. patient begins
from the moment he arrives in the admitting room.

00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:25.180
Everything that is done for him makes him
feel that the people in the hospital are friendly

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:26.250
and want to help.

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:31.440
Your manner, approach, and the way you handle
N.P. patients is of great importance.

00:03:31.820 --> 00:03:35.360
Remember, they're more sensitive than those
who are physically ill.

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:37.820
[Speaker 2:] Right this way.

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:39.100
These are papers on Miller, sir.

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:39.720
[Speaker 2:] Thank you.

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:40.560
That's all.

00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:43.320
Have a chair.

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:55.860
Your name is Miller?

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:00.500
How are you feeling, Miller?

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:03.000
[Miller:] Terrible.

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:06.860
I didn't sleep for three days.

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:09.820
[Speaker 2:] Well we'll try and help you get
some sleep here.

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:12.740
[Narrator:] This man looks dazed and slowed
down.

00:04:13.220 --> 00:04:16.680
A condition which can develop either in boot
camp or in combat.

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:21.620
You've been in the dumps too, but you've always managed to snap out of it, because that's normal.

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:26.580
This man can't without help, any more than
he could set his arm if it were broken, or

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:27.920
take out his own appendix.

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:32.780
A smart corpsman can give him that help and
start him on the way to recovery.

00:04:34.780 --> 00:04:35.740
[Speaker 3:] How about some chow?

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:39.840
Aw, c'mon, it looks pretty good to me.

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:46.380
[Miller:] I shouldn't be here.

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:49.520
I should be back with the ship.

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:52.880
I have no business here.

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:02.580
[Speaker 3:] You know Miller, the quickest
way to get back to the boys is by eatin' your chow.

00:05:07.460 --> 00:05:08.040
Tastes good!

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:09.820
Here.

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:11.880
Try one.

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:14.380
Go on, try one.

00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:34.660
Tastes good doesn't it?

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:36.320
Try some milk now.

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:43.500
[Miller:] I gotta get back to the ship.

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:46.440
[Speaker 3:] Sure, but the milk will taste
good with those spuds.

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:49.520
[Narrator:] This corpsman is doing a darn
good job.

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:53.340
He is firm, but kind, so as not to irritate
the patient.

00:05:53.780 --> 00:05:58.480
The next time it will be easier for both of
them, and the patient has made another step to recovery.

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:04.700
While Miller is having his first square meal
in days, let's get a few things straight about N.P. patients.

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:08.100
First of all, the patient is never batty or
nuts.

00:06:08.380 --> 00:06:10.820
He is sick and must be treated as such.

00:06:11.180 --> 00:06:11.880
Take Miller.

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:16.100
He isn't stubborn or unreasonable because
he doesn't want to eat.

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:21.040
That's just a symptom of his particular illness
and the corpsman used the right approach.

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:23.640
Not all N.P. patients will be the same.

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:28.440
There are many different types of mental illness, and we are going to show you a few

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:29.480
you will be seeing most.

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:31.880
Some will be slowed down, like Miller.

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:37.300
Others, tense and on edge, like cases of psychoneurosis
or combat fatigue.

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:43.980
You will see the unreliable psychopath, the
very depressed, suicidal patient, and the catatonic.

00:06:45.340 --> 00:06:51.240
Some may be mixed up and excited, others overactive
with more energy than they can use.

00:06:51.620 --> 00:06:57.020
In each case, the symptoms will be different
and all require different treatment.

00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:02.060
It is the corpsman, rather than the doctor,
who will be with the patient most of the time.

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:08.160
And for that reason, the corpsman must know his patients, watch their behavior and report

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:09.319
anything unusual.

00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:12.860
The corpsman is the eyes and ears of the doctor.

00:07:14.020 --> 00:07:15.020
Here's what I mean.

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:17.000
How did Harris get along last night?

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:21.300
I'm afraid he didn't get much sleep, even
with the sodium amytal you prescribed.

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:24.240
He seems awfully restless and confused this
morning.

00:07:24.620 --> 00:07:26.600
Hmm, better have him see me the first thing
after sick call.

00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:28.080
How's Miller today?

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:30.020
Oh, he's getting along fine, sir.

00:07:30.260 --> 00:07:32.660
He ate everything we gave him and wanted more.

00:07:32.980 --> 00:07:36.240
[Narrator:] Information about the patient's
behavior is being given to the doctor so he

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:37.760
can follow the case closely.

00:07:38.060 --> 00:07:39.240
Take Miller for instance.

00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:44.500
The doctor is now ordering him a needle shower and spray, and hydrotherapy as part of his treatment.

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:49.420
[Sounds of water running in shower.]

00:07:49.420 --> 00:07:51.460
[Corpsman operating water hoses:] Step back a little, will you, Miller?

00:07:55.160 --> 00:08:13.480
[Sound of water spraying powerfully on Miller's
back.]

00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:20.560
[Narrator:] There is another type of patient
you will see, and probably quite a few.

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:26.000
Some will be in hospitals, but most will be
treated in naval convalescent centers.

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:31.660
This type of patient may not seem particularly
sick to you, but he may be a psychopath,

00:08:31.660 --> 00:08:35.260
a case of psychoneurosis, or combat fatigue.

00:08:35.620 --> 00:08:42.080
This kind of patient is usually tense, nervous, jittery, and often makes a tough problem to manage.

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:45.280
Take combat fatigue, for example.

00:08:47.340 --> 00:08:48.540
[Corpsman Al:] Get up, Mac, get up!

00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:49.700
[Mac:] What the hell you think you're doing?

00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:51.980
[Al:] You're supposed to hit the deck with
the others and I'm here to see that you do.

00:08:51.980 --> 00:08:52.700
[Corpsman Bob:] What's the trouble?

00:08:52.700 --> 00:08:53.980
[Mac:] This jerk's trying to get me up.

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:57.540
You know I can't sleep nights, and when I
finally do fall asleep he tries to push me out, for God's sake.

00:08:58.360 --> 00:08:59.480
[Bob:] Aw, take it easy, fella.

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:01.300
Al here is new, he won't do it again.

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:05.220
But don't forget, you're still in the service
and we've got rules here whether we like them

00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:05.840
or not.

00:09:06.300 --> 00:09:08.899
Besides, you're going to get well a lot quicker
if you follow the gang.

00:09:09.260 --> 00:09:10.200
Now get out there with your mates.

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:11.300
You've missed breakfast already.

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:15.100
[All:] I don't see why we have to baby these
goldbricks.

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:16.280
[Bob:] He's no goldbrick.

00:09:16.280 --> 00:09:17.360
He won a unit citation.

00:09:17.960 --> 00:09:20.360
You don't have to baby them, just use your
head.

00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:21.480
Now watch this.

00:09:26.560 --> 00:09:27.560
That may help.

00:09:28.820 --> 00:09:30.340
Time to get up, Ed.

00:09:30.340 --> 00:09:31.840
Ed, time to get up.

00:09:31.960 --> 00:09:32.960
[Ed:] What's the matter?

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:34.080
[Bob:] Why, everybody's up and out.

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:35.160
You've missed breakfast.

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:36.440
[Ed:] Aw, nuts.

00:09:36.440 --> 00:09:38.080
[Bob:] If you hurry, you can get a cup of
coffee.

00:09:38.860 --> 00:09:40.440
[Al:] Maybe you've got something there.

00:09:41.860 --> 00:09:43.780
[Bob:] They probably couldn't sleep last night.

00:09:44.060 --> 00:09:48.120
Lots of things patients do here seem like
plain goldbricking and bellyaching, but they're

00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:48.620
not.

00:09:49.180 --> 00:09:50.920
They're symptoms of whatever they've got.

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:56.040
They may act nasty and insulting at times,
but just keep your sense of humor

00:09:56.260 --> 00:10:00.620
and don't forget that if you get tough with
them, they'll make your job that much harder.

00:10:00.860 --> 00:10:01.820
[Al:] I guess you're right.

00:10:02.740 --> 00:10:04.440
[Bob:] Same goes for the psychopath.

00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:07.630
He can be a real troublemaker and requires
plenty of watching.

00:10:07.630 --> 00:10:09.160
I nearly got fooled by one yesterday.

00:10:12.220 --> 00:10:13.700
[Kern:] Hello, Bob, check me out, will you?

00:10:13.700 --> 00:10:14.360
[Bob:] Why sure.

00:10:14.360 --> 00:10:15.280
Have you got your liberty pass?

00:10:15.540 --> 00:10:18.560
[Kern:] Well, uh Doctor Adams said he'd have someone leave it for me at the main gate.

00:10:18.780 --> 00:10:19.480
I just saw him.

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:20.980
[Bob:] He said it'd be all right?

00:10:20.980 --> 00:10:21.640
[Kern:] Sure.

00:10:21.640 --> 00:10:22.540
'Til six tonight.

00:10:22.860 --> 00:10:25.260
Hey, I'll get to see a double-header today.

00:10:25.270 --> 00:10:26.350
[Bob:] Just a minute fella.

00:10:26.350 --> 00:10:27.740
I'd better check with Doctor Adams.

00:10:27.740 --> 00:10:29.340
[Kern:] Oh, well Doctor Adams won't be in...

00:10:32.580 --> 00:10:33.620
[Bob:] Hello, Doctor Adams.

00:10:33.880 --> 00:10:35.940
Why, this is Davis in ward seven, sir.

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:38.760
Did you tell Kern he could leave the hospital
today, sir?

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:40.740
I see.

00:10:42.320 --> 00:10:42.980
Aye, aye, sir.

00:10:44.120 --> 00:10:45.740
Sorry, Doctor Adams says no.

00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:48.220
[Kern:] I don't understand it.

00:10:48.220 --> 00:10:49.380
I just left him.

00:10:50.020 --> 00:10:52.580
Who the hell does he think he is, anyway?

00:10:56.900 --> 00:11:00.840
[Narrator:] Well that patient was a pretty
good liar, but a little challenged when he was caught.

00:11:01.460 --> 00:11:05.540
You've probably seen psychopaths just like
that, outside of hospitals too.

00:11:05.800 --> 00:11:09.700
Because there are lots of them, and lying
is only one of their symptoms.

00:11:10.640 --> 00:11:15.140
One of the most important parts of the corpsman's
work is the protection of patients.

00:11:15.320 --> 00:11:19.060
Sometimes from others, and sometimes from
themselves.

00:11:19.360 --> 00:11:23.800
If the patient is depressed enough, he may
even attempt suicide.

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:27.760
That is why he may be put in a room with a
special watch.

00:11:27.940 --> 00:11:30.600
Not for punishment, but for protection.

00:11:32.140 --> 00:11:32.640
[Roberts:] No use.

00:11:32.860 --> 00:11:33.980
It's no use going on.

00:11:34.760 --> 00:11:36.300
I ought to be dead.

00:11:36.300 --> 00:11:38.360
Oh my God, it's no use at all.

00:11:38.800 --> 00:11:40.180
[Corpsman assisting him:] Is there anything
I can do for you?

00:11:40.180 --> 00:11:43.540
[Roberts:] Oh my God, nothing anybody can
do for me.

00:11:44.260 --> 00:11:47.300
I don't rate it, I don't rate anything in
the whole world.

00:11:47.700 --> 00:11:49.280
[Corpsman assisting:] Well, is there anything I can get for you?

00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:55.940
[Roberts:] No...I, I would like my mother's
picture that I brought here with me.

00:11:56.000 --> 00:11:58.220
[Corpsman assisting:] All right, then I'll
get it for you right away.

00:12:01.940 --> 00:12:05.000
Roberts up on F-6 said he has his mother's
pictures in his gear, you want to look it

00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:05.700
up for me please?

00:12:05.840 --> 00:12:06.340
[Inventory man at desk:] Sure.

00:12:10.840 --> 00:12:12.280
[Corpsman assisting:] He wants the picture
pretty bad.

00:12:14.440 --> 00:12:16.700
Poor kid, feels about as low as he can.

00:12:17.780 --> 00:12:19.160
I hope this picture helps him some.

00:12:19.900 --> 00:12:20.400
[Inventory man:] Roberts?

00:12:21.100 --> 00:12:23.280
Isn't he the one we're supposed to keep an
eye on?

00:12:23.280 --> 00:12:24.280
[Corpsman assisting:] Yeah, that's the guy.

00:12:27.540 --> 00:12:28.660
[Inventory man:] Is he alone now?

00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:30.140
[Corpsman assisting:] I suppose so.

00:12:30.140 --> 00:12:31.580
I just come down here for a minute.

00:12:32.380 --> 00:12:34.800
[Inventory man:] Yeah, but it might be the
wrong minute.

00:12:35.440 --> 00:12:37.200
He could kill himself in that minute.

00:12:37.920 --> 00:12:41.980
That man is supposed to get a shock treatment this morning, and he has a darn good chance of getting well.

00:12:43.740 --> 00:12:48.640
Oh, he probably wants his mother's picture
all right, but he might want this glass, too.

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:50.880
And broken glass can be awful sharp.

00:12:51.680 --> 00:12:53.080
You better get back up there on the double.

00:12:53.080 --> 00:12:54.100
[Corpsman assisting:] Okay.

00:12:55.820 --> 00:12:59.440
[Narrator:] When men are sick and depressed
like Roberts, you've got to be on a constant

00:12:59.440 --> 00:13:02.340
lookout for anything they could use to hurt
themselves.

00:13:02.540 --> 00:13:08.300
Glass in any form, medicine bottles, eyeglasses,
or anything that will make a sharp edge.

00:13:08.740 --> 00:13:11.540
Remove all belts, neckerchiefs, scarves.

00:13:12.140 --> 00:13:15.680
Watch out for knives, razor blades, or anything
that will cut.

00:13:16.060 --> 00:13:18.480
You may never see a patient as depressed as
that.

00:13:18.640 --> 00:13:21.800
But if you do, remember Roberts and his mother's
picture.

00:13:22.380 --> 00:13:26.400
Fortunately for him, there are several ways
of treating his condition.

00:13:26.680 --> 00:13:29.940
One of the most successful is the electroshock
treatment.

00:13:30.940 --> 00:13:31.480
[Roberts:] Oh!

00:13:32.960 --> 00:13:34.900
Oh my God!

00:13:35.420 --> 00:13:36.280
I can't.

00:13:37.720 --> 00:13:38.720
I don't.....uhhhh.

00:13:43.320 --> 00:13:44.920
[Supervisign doctor:] This will help you to
get well.

00:13:44.920 --> 00:13:47.760
[Roberts:] Nothing will help me to get well.

00:13:48.720 --> 00:13:49.720
Ohhhh...

00:13:52.240 --> 00:13:52.840
[Supervising doctor:] Ready?

00:13:54.620 --> 00:13:56.440
[Roberts screams, then convulses as the shock
treatment is administered.

00:13:57.840 --> 00:14:22.700
He trembles and moans.

00:14:22.900 --> 00:14:43.100
Medical staff assist the patient.]

00:14:44.240 --> 00:14:49.180
[A manual lawnmower creaks as it's pushed
across the lawn.]

00:14:49.260 --> 00:14:50.940
[Bob:] Kind of hot pushing that thing around,
isn't it?

00:14:51.240 --> 00:14:52.200
[Roberts:] Oh not too bad.

00:14:52.280 --> 00:14:53.520
The exercise feels good.

00:14:53.840 --> 00:14:55.240
[Bob:] How're the treatments going along?

00:14:55.260 --> 00:14:56.440
[Roberts:] Oh I'm through with them.

00:14:56.440 --> 00:14:58.760
Had six, the doctor says I don't need anymore.

00:14:59.260 --> 00:15:02.200
Guess I was pretty sick when I started taking
those treatments.

00:15:02.200 --> 00:15:03.459
I don't even remember.

00:15:04.000 --> 00:15:06.060
[Bob:] Well, it's good to see you looking
so well.

00:15:10.420 --> 00:15:13.740
[Narrator:] It's things like that that give
you kick out of working around an N.P. ward.

00:15:14.320 --> 00:15:17.480
To know you helped that man recover makes
you feel good.

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:21.040
Of course, not all patients will recover so
rapidly.

00:15:21.520 --> 00:15:26.160
A lot of them will may take weeks or even
months, so don't get impatient.

00:15:26.960 --> 00:15:30.500
Another type of patient you may help treat
is the catatonic.

00:15:35.820 --> 00:15:41.580
He may stay like this for hours, or days without
moving, eating, or even speaking.

00:15:42.560 --> 00:15:45.360
But he hears and understands everything.

00:15:54.020 --> 00:15:55.660
[Another corpsman assigned to help catatonic man:] All right Harris, we're going to see the doctor now.

00:16:13.280 --> 00:16:15.580
Okay Harris, we're just going down the hall.

00:16:18.040 --> 00:16:19.340
[Doctor:] Is that seven and half grams?

00:16:19.340 --> 00:16:19.840
[Nurse:] Yes sir.

00:16:31.060 --> 00:16:31.940
[Corpsman:] Take it easy, Harris.

00:16:31.980 --> 00:16:33.300
The doctor wants to talk to you.

00:16:40.840 --> 00:16:42.000
[Doctor:] Sodium amytal please.

00:16:50.140 --> 00:16:52.300
It's all right, Harris, this will make you
feel better.

00:16:53.500 --> 00:16:56.580
This medicine will help you to relax so you
can talk to us.

00:16:59.020 --> 00:17:01.400
All you'll feel is the prick of the needle.

00:17:05.600 --> 00:17:06.380
Tourniquet off.

00:17:18.540 --> 00:17:20.340
I want you to talk to us, Harris.

00:17:30.400 --> 00:17:32.880
We want to help you, but you won't tell us
what's wrong.

00:17:35.920 --> 00:17:38.400
You know we want to help you to get well,
don't you?

00:17:40.040 --> 00:17:42.320
[Harris:] I know everything happening here.

00:17:42.640 --> 00:17:43.660
[Doctor:] That's fine.

00:17:44.260 --> 00:17:47.300
Then tell us what's worrying you and why you can't talk.

00:17:48.020 --> 00:17:49.460
[Harris:] They tell me not to.

00:17:50.120 --> 00:17:51.340
[Doctor:] Who tells you not to?

00:17:52.440 --> 00:17:52.940
[Harris:] The voices.

00:17:55.080 --> 00:17:56.760
[Doctor:] You see, you can talk.

00:17:58.260 --> 00:17:59.640
[Harris:]I guess I can.

00:18:01.000 --> 00:18:02.420
[Doctor:] About these voices.

00:18:02.900 --> 00:18:03.900
When do you hear them?

00:18:06.040 --> 00:18:09.340
[Harris:] Mostly at night, just as I go to
bed.

00:18:09.980 --> 00:18:12.200
[Doctor:] Are they male, or female voices?

00:18:12.200 --> 00:18:12.820
[Harris:] Male.

00:18:13.560 --> 00:18:15.380
There's one that keeps threatening me.

00:18:16.760 --> 00:18:20.160
[Narrator:] Starting with this treatment,
and helped by various others, the patient

00:18:20.160 --> 00:18:21.600
will continue to get better,

00:18:21.760 --> 00:18:23.740
sometimes in a matter of weeks.

00:18:31.840 --> 00:18:33.360
[Bob:] Well, how's everything today, Harris?

00:18:33.360 --> 00:18:33.900
[Harris:] Okay!

00:18:33.960 --> 00:18:34.700
Got a light?

00:18:34.740 --> 00:18:35.240
[Bob:] Sure.

00:18:42.140 --> 00:18:44.120
[Harris:] Was I really nuts when they brought me here?

00:18:44.800 --> 00:18:45.700
[Bob:] Well, what do you think?

00:18:46.200 --> 00:18:47.520
[Harris:] Guess I was pretty sick.

00:18:47.980 --> 00:18:48.800
[Bob:] Yes you were.

00:18:48.800 --> 00:18:49.540
Plenty sick.

00:18:49.800 --> 00:18:51.710
It must be nice to feel like yourself again.

00:18:52.020 --> 00:18:52.880
[Harris:] It sure is!

00:18:53.300 --> 00:18:55.940
When do you think I can go downtown to see
a football game?

00:18:56.420 --> 00:18:59.820
[Bob:] Well, I don't know, but I'll talk to
the doctor about it today.

00:19:00.020 --> 00:19:00.520
[Harris:] Swell!

00:19:00.520 --> 00:19:01.020
Thanks.

00:19:05.800 --> 00:19:09.000
[Narrator:] The corpsman played a large part in the recovery of that patient, and still does.

00:19:09.900 --> 00:19:12.680
He was honest, and not too optimistic in his
answers.

00:19:13.300 --> 00:19:16.180
You'll get questions all day long from patients.

00:19:16.180 --> 00:19:21.090
So if you don't know the answer, the safest
thing to say is 'I don't know,

00:19:21.090 --> 00:19:22.200
but I'll try and find out.

00:19:23.640 --> 00:19:28.800
One of the most serious problems on the N.P. ward is the mixed-up patient who becomes excited

00:19:28.800 --> 00:19:29.800
and assaultive.

00:19:34.840 --> 00:19:36.040
[Officer:] Good morning, Charlie.

00:19:36.260 --> 00:19:37.480
[Charlie:] Well, go ahead.

00:19:37.760 --> 00:19:38.920
Go ahead and shoot me.

00:19:39.360 --> 00:19:40.800
[Officer:] I'm not going to shoot you, Charlie.

00:19:40.980 --> 00:19:41.900
[Charlie:] Oh yes you are.

00:19:42.200 --> 00:19:43.900
You're a policeman and it's your duty.

00:19:44.260 --> 00:19:45.260
[Officer:] C'mon let's go.

00:19:45.260 --> 00:19:46.390
It's time you took a little rest.

00:19:46.390 --> 00:19:47.780
[Charlie:] No, I can't rest.

00:19:47.780 --> 00:19:48.990
I deserve to be shot.

00:19:49.520 --> 00:19:50.440
[Officer:] No you don't Charlie.

00:19:50.700 --> 00:19:51.900
Come along, let's lie down.

00:19:51.910 --> 00:19:53.420
[Charlie:] You've got to shoot me.

00:19:53.420 --> 00:19:54.420
I'm yellow!

00:19:54.420 --> 00:20:05.480
[Sounds of a scuffle as Charlie yells and
attacks the officer.]

00:20:06.240 --> 00:20:06.980
[Officer:] It's all right, Charlie.

00:20:06.980 --> 00:20:07.480
Take it easy.

00:20:07.480 --> 00:20:08.060
[Bill:] Can I help?

00:20:08.060 --> 00:20:09.180
[Officer:] Yeah, open the door, Bill.

00:20:10.080 --> 00:20:14.040
[Narrator:] No matter how tough and unreasonable
a patient is, he must never be manhandled.

00:20:14.700 --> 00:20:18.300
If you are ever in a spot like this, get as
much help as you can.

00:20:18.720 --> 00:20:23.200
Lift the patient off his feet, take off his
shoes, and carry him into a strong room.

00:20:23.480 --> 00:20:25.440
Then call the doctor immediately.

00:20:31.840 --> 00:20:33.840
[Click of door being unlocked.]

00:20:39.400 --> 00:20:40.800
[Doctor:] What seems to be the trouble, Jones?

00:20:43.900 --> 00:20:45.300
Don't you want to talk to me about it?

00:20:47.740 --> 00:20:49.440
Better get him down to hydro for a cold pack.

00:20:49.440 --> 00:20:49.940
[Corpsman assisting doctor:] Yes sir.

00:21:05.240 --> 00:21:06.440
[Older man in street clothes:] How do you
feel this morning?

00:21:06.440 --> 00:21:07.520
[Charlie:] Not so good.

00:21:07.760 --> 00:21:10.120
[Man:] Well, the doctor's ordered a treatment
for you again this morning.

00:21:10.120 --> 00:21:11.900
You'll feel better after you get your treatment.

00:21:23.900 --> 00:21:26.100
Get him good and tight down to the foot, please.

00:21:49.860 --> 00:21:51.160
Want to raise your head, Charlie?

00:22:01.060 --> 00:22:01.660
All right.

00:22:04.620 --> 00:22:05.340
How's that?

00:22:05.420 --> 00:22:06.160
Comfortable?

00:22:06.340 --> 00:22:07.880
[Charlie:] Feels pretty good, thanks.

00:22:08.340 --> 00:22:10.200
I'm going to go to sleep now.

00:22:18.700 --> 00:22:20.800
[Narrator:] There's another type of overactive
patient.

00:22:20.800 --> 00:22:22.340
But he is not so mixed up.

00:22:22.720 --> 00:22:26.780
He has more energy than he can use sensibly
and takes careful handling.

00:22:27.020 --> 00:22:31.600
Like most N.P. patients, if handled improperly,
he will behave improperly.

00:22:31.920 --> 00:22:36.820
If you forget that he is a patient and you
are a corpsman, anything can happen.

00:22:42.660 --> 00:22:44.620
[Bennett:] Say, these aren't the magazines
you promised me.

00:22:44.620 --> 00:22:45.620
Where they hell are they?

00:22:45.750 --> 00:22:48.740
[Corpsman at desk:] For about the tenth time,
Bennett, I'm telling you I'll see about them

00:22:48.740 --> 00:22:50.040
as soon as I possibly can.

00:22:50.060 --> 00:22:51.900
Now don't bother me, I'm busy!

00:22:57.380 --> 00:23:20.640
[Dramatic music and the sound of glass breaking.]

00:23:24.200 --> 00:23:28.080
[Narrator:] This patient can be calmed down
by continuous tub and hydrotherapy.

00:23:30.960 --> 00:23:32.500
[Chief doctor:] How's the temperature running
Jim?

00:23:32.700 --> 00:23:34.500
[Corpsman in hydro room:] It's running about
98 degrees, chief.

00:23:36.000 --> 00:23:37.720
[Chief:] Better raise to a hundred.

00:23:37.720 --> 00:23:40.080
The man's been pretty excited this morning.

00:23:40.300 --> 00:23:40.800
[Hydro corpsman:] Okay.

00:23:48.240 --> 00:23:52.240
[Narrator:] Now all his extra horsepower can
be used on something constructive and helpful.

00:23:52.420 --> 00:24:08.940
[Music]

00:24:09.300 --> 00:24:13.380
To get the patient back into an organized
routine where he feels himself a member

00:24:13.380 --> 00:24:15.500
of the group is another part of your job.

00:24:16.320 --> 00:24:20.520
Under the supervision of the doctor, occupational
therapist, and corpsman,

00:24:20.520 --> 00:24:24.420
organized activities like these will help
most patients recover.

00:24:25.160 --> 00:24:55.200
[Music]

00:24:56.940 --> 00:25:01.880
I haven't got time to show you in detail the
many things you may be doing as an N.P. corpsman,

00:25:01.880 --> 00:25:03.500
but I'd like to include a few.

00:25:05.100 --> 00:25:10.880
You may help the doctor in the EEG room by
taking a record of the patient's brainwaves.

00:25:11.820 --> 00:25:15.300
The corpsman is checking the electrodes before
making a record.

00:25:16.760 --> 00:25:21.900
These tracings often give the doctor valuable
information in certain brain disorders.

00:25:26.240 --> 00:25:30.660
You will be expected to take the patient to
the psychologist, where careful tests are

00:25:30.660 --> 00:25:34.160
are given to measure intelligence or test certain skills.

00:25:36.140 --> 00:25:39.020
You may have to take him across the country
on a train.

00:25:41.880 --> 00:25:42.780
Aboard a ship.

00:25:46.940 --> 00:25:47.940
Or in a plane.

00:25:48.780 --> 00:25:52.900
Under travel conditions, what you have learned
becomes more important than ever.

00:25:53.240 --> 00:25:58.980
There is about five times as much chance for
suicide, escape, or injury while traveling.

00:25:59.260 --> 00:26:03.680
Remember, here you're on your own and don't
have the protection of the hospital ward to

00:26:03.680 --> 00:26:04.600
help you.

00:26:07.740 --> 00:26:10.400
When he gets better, you may have to take
him shopping.

00:26:12.480 --> 00:26:15.280
Or you may take several patients to a ball
game.

00:26:16.700 --> 00:26:17.780
Or the theater.

00:26:19.840 --> 00:26:24.320
Your job may seem tough at times, but men
with your special training are needed.

00:26:24.760 --> 00:26:28.040
When your shipmates leave the hospital happy
and well again,

00:26:28.040 --> 00:26:31.600
there's a lot of satisfaction in knowing you've
done your part.

00:26:32.880 --> 00:26:33.920
[Corpsman at desk:] Here's your wristwatch.

00:26:33.920 --> 00:26:34.620
[Miller:] Thanks!

00:26:35.180 --> 00:26:36.500
You know I got a 30-day leave.

00:26:36.640 --> 00:26:37.640
[Desk corpsman:] Glad to be going, eh?

00:26:37.840 --> 00:26:38.340
[Miller:] You bet!

00:26:38.600 --> 00:26:40.440
Not that you fellas haven't been all right.

00:26:40.760 --> 00:26:41.260
[Desk corpsman:] Thanks.

00:26:41.580 --> 00:26:43.340
Here's something else that came in yesterday.

00:26:43.560 --> 00:26:44.980
You must've left it on the train.

00:26:44.980 --> 00:26:46.380
[Miller:] Say that's swell.

00:26:46.580 --> 00:26:48.380
You guys never forget things do ya?

00:26:48.660 --> 00:26:50.800
This'll come in handy on that 30-day leave.

00:26:50.800 --> 00:26:51.580
[Desk corpsman:] Yeah.

00:26:51.720 --> 00:26:52.220
[Miller:] Well, thanks.

00:26:52.820 --> 00:26:53.840
Thanks for everything.

00:26:53.840 --> 00:26:54.340
[Desk corpsman:] Sure.

00:26:54.340 --> 00:26:54.900
Good luck.

00:26:54.900 --> 00:26:55.660
[Miller:] Be seeing you.

00:26:55.780 --> 00:26:56.280
[Desk corpsman:] Right-o.

00:26:57.400 --> 00:27:34.020
[Music]

